<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Lucca and Piombino</h1><p class="st-meta">1805 CE – 1815 CE · capital Lucca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tuscan principality Napoleon created for his sister Elisa Bonaparte, who governed it as an energetic reforming ruler.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Republic of Lucca</a> and joined it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Principality of Piombino</a>, granting the new state to his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa</a> and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felice-baciocchi">Felice Baciocchi</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-massa-and-carrara">Massa and Carrara</a> were added in 1806.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa</a>, not her husband, held real power. She introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, closed many convents, reorganised schools and finances, and promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> marble industry, founding an academy and workshops that produced busts of the Bonaparte family for all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. She patronised the violinist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-paganini">Niccolò Paganini</a> at her court. In 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> also made her Grand Duchess of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa</a> fled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s empire collapsed in 1814. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-luisa-duchess-of-lucca">Maria Luisa of Bourbon</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Duchy of Lucca</a>, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Piombino</a> went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-lucca-and-piombino.jpg" alt="Principality of Lucca and Piombino" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Hierakares · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Principality_of_Lucca_(1805-1809).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1805 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elisa Bonaparte, Felice Baciocchi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lucca and Piombino</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=principality-of-lucca-and-piombino" aria-label="Principality of Lucca and Piombino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4932 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Lucca and Piombino lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5252 880L5252 880M5252 880L5254 909M5252 880L5054 728M5252 880L5252 880"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5252 880h0M5254 909h0M5054 728h0M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lucca. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=principality-of-lucca-and-piombino">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
